### Fan Hardcores

**Fan Hardcores** are web series that are inspired by PeanutButterGamer's Hardcore series. Individual seasons from these series are often referred to simply as **Hardcores**. They typically follow the same premise of PBG HC (premise can be found on <a href="/shrines/pbg-hardcore" data-sveltekit-noscroll>About</a> page). A list of almost every Hardcore ever released can be found [here](https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1IO-eYlzTSWKRxw0iGTq4eSmJG9-ggdAfFC8h9b5Zaz0/edit?usp=sharing) (big shoutout to [Woodzee](https://hardcore.wiki/wiki/Woodzee) for creating this).

---

### History

The earliest known Hardcores actually predate the release of the first season of PBG HC. The first known Hardcore is [MindCrack Ultra Hardcore #1](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YZPPTyDcrxM), which released its first episode on February 22, 2012. From years 2012 to 2016, very few Hardcores besides PBG HC were created. A notable fan Hardcore series from these years is [Funcore](https://hardcore.wiki/wiki/Funcore).

After the return of PBG HC with the release of Minecraft #5 in December of 2016, Hardcore production began to rise rapidly. It is as this time where most people would say that the Hardcore community was birthed. In February of 2017, Hardcore community member [Noshiro](<https://hardcore.wiki/wiki/Noah_(Ultra_Hardcore)>) founded a private friend group for recording Hardcores and hosting "casuals" (non-recorded Hardcores that people participate in for fun) called [Nova Quest](https://hardcore.wiki/wiki/Nova_Quest) (Nova for short). Membership in Nova was a sign of status in the community, and getting a Nova member in your Hardcore was highly sought after, so much so that the term "Nova clout" was created to refer to the attention your Hardcore would receive if you got a member of Nova in it. <SmallText>(if this sounds like a very stupid concept that only a bunch of the 13-year-olds would believe in, then you would be correct)</SmallText>. In July of 2017, the [Hardcore community discord server](https://discord.gg/PtNk5BYg) was created.

On August 18th, 2017, Nova member [Simoz](https://hardcore.wiki/wiki/Simoz) released the first episode of his third full-length Hardcore season, [Minecraft #2](https://www.youtube.com/playlist?list=PLteZ5fMk51_1ECWnCmSquyb-RTmJc-M3n). This particular Hardcore is significant because it is at this point where fan Hardcore series began to contend PBG HC in quality and entertainment. [Simoz's Hardcore series](https://hardcore.wiki/wiki/Simoz%27s_Hardcore_Series) quickly became one the most popular in the community. In November of 2017, community member <BlurredText>NodOff</BlurredText> uploaded a [video](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WZxX2LkA4lc) advertising the community discord server and the newly created [Hardcore wiki](https://hardcore.wiki/wiki/Hardcore_Wiki). This video was how I and potentially others found the HC community. Other notable fan HC series from around this time include [Cri's HC](https://hardcore.wiki/wiki/ICryInMySleep%27s_Hardcore_Series), [SyKore](https://hardcore.wiki/wiki/SyKore), [EXTREME](https://hardcore.wiki/wiki/EXTREME), and [ToastCore](https://hardcore.wiki/wiki/ToastCore).

In April of 2018, Simoz uploaded the first episode of his fifth season, Minecraft #2. This season is regarded as one of the best HCs of all time, though it does not hold up today as great as it did in 2018. Also in April of 2018, XanyLeaves uploaded the first epsiode of the debut season of his fan HC series [EPICORE](https://hardcore.wiki/wiki/EPICORE). This fan HC series became so popular that a community of people entirely indepedent from the greater HC community formed around just this series. Other notable Hardcores created in Nothing super notable happened in 2019 despite the creation of a few popular HCs such as [Amber K.'s HC](https://hardcore.wiki/wiki/Amber_K.%27s_Hardcore_Series), [Bonus HC](https://hardcore.wiki/wiki/Bonus_HC), and [Team Scallop](https://hardcore.wiki/wiki/Team_Scallop).

Heading into the 2020s, the community began to experience a shift, in that people began to mature and realize a lot of the things that the community did were dumb, like believing in "Nova clout". People also began to realize that saying slurs and making insensitive jokes in their HCs was #NotCool! The HC community became a much less toxic place to be in and almost all HCs past 2021 actually hold up well thanks to this shift.

In 2020, [the Hardcore Awards](https://hardcore.wiki/wiki/The_Hardcore_Awards) were created. Amber K.'s [Secret Rivals](https://hardcore.wiki/wiki/Amber_K.%27s_Hardcore/Minecraft_Secret_Rivals) won Season of the Year in 2020. In late 2021, the Boiled Peanuts release their fifth Minecraft season. This season won Season of the Year in 2021 and is widely regarded as the best HC ever, including PBG HC. In January of 2022, Non Copyright Infringing Channel (NCIC) published the season Very Big Core, a MineZ HC with a full cast of 30 people, making it one of the biggest HCs in terms of cast size. [Detonation 2](https://hardcore.wiki/wiki/Bonus_HC/Detonation_2), Bonus HC's fourth full-length season, won Season of the Year in 2022.

In 2023, the first Hardcore Jam, a competition where members of the community are challenged to make a short Hardcore within a set time limit and under other constraints such as a specific theme, was hosted. A list of all the Hardcore Jams that have been hosted can be found [here](https://hardcore.wiki/wiki/Hardcore_Jams#List_of_Hardcore_Jams).
